  "dom_id": "step-q7644",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Calculate the points where the ellipse \n$\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis.",
  "worked_solution": "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ 3+(y+3)^{2} & =12 \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y+3 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =-3\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =-6\\,,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,-6)$ and $(0,0).$",
